We need to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer from a club of 20 members, where no member can hold more than one office. This is a permutation problem because the order matters (the roles are distinct: president, vice-president, and treasurer).  We have 20 choices for the president.  Once the president is chosen, we have 19 choices left for the vice-president.  After the president and vice-president are chosen, we have 18 choices left for the treasurer.

The total number of ways to select the three officers is the product of the number of choices for each position: $20 \times 19 \times 18$.

Now we calculate the product: $20 \times 19 \times 18 = 20 \times (19 \times 18) = 20 \times 342 = 6840$.

The number of ways to select the officers is $20 \times 19 \times 18 = 6840$.

