Let $w = z^3.$ Then the equation $z^6 + z^3 + 1 = 0$ becomes $w^2 + w + 1 = 0.$ By the quadratic formula,
$$w = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \frac{-1 \pm i \sqrt{3}}{2}.$$
We can write these roots in polar form as
$$w = e^{i(2\pi/3)} = \cos \frac{2\pi}{3} + i \sin \frac{2\pi}{3} = -\frac{1}{2} + i \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$$
and
$$w = e^{i(4\pi/3)} = \cos \frac{4\pi}{3} + i \sin \frac{4\pi}{3} = -\frac{1}{2} - i \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$
Thus, $z^3 = e^{i(2\pi/3)}$ or $z^3 = e^{i(4\pi/3)}.$

If $z^3 = e^{i(2\pi/3)},$ then $z = e^{i(2\pi/9 + 2k\pi/3)}$ for $k = 0, 1, 2.$ Then the roots are
\begin{align*} z &= e^{i(2\pi/9)} \\ z &= e^{i(2\pi/9 + 2\pi/3)} = e^{i(8\pi/9)} \\ z &= e^{i(2\pi/9 + 4\pi/3)} = e^{i(14\pi/9)} \end{align*}
If $z^3 = e^{i(4\pi/3)},$ then $z = e^{i(4\pi/9 + 2k\pi/3)}$ for $k = 0, 1, 2.$ Then the roots are
\begin{align*} z &= e^{i(4\pi/9)} \\ z &= e^{i(4\pi/9 + 2\pi/3)} = e^{i(10\pi/9)} \\ z &= e^{i(4\pi/9 + 4\pi/3)} = e^{i(16\pi/9)} \end{align*}
The possible arguments of the roots are $\frac{2\pi}{9},$ $\frac{4\pi}{9},$ $\frac{8\pi}{9},$ $\frac{10\pi}{9},$ $\frac{14\pi}{9},$ $\frac{16\pi}{9}.$ We want the argument that is between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ,$ i.e. between $\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $\pi.$
The angles in radians are
\begin{align*} \frac{2\pi}{9} & \approx 40^\circ \\ \frac{4\pi}{9} & \approx 80^\circ \\ \frac{8\pi}{9} & \approx 160^\circ \\ \frac{10\pi}{9} & \approx 200^\circ \\ \frac{14\pi}{9} & \approx 280^\circ \\ \frac{16\pi}{9} & \approx 320^\circ \end{align*}
The only angle between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$ is $\frac{8\pi}{9},$ which is $160^\circ.$

